		<article class="max-w-4xl space-y-12 font-sans border-t border-mapa-contour/60 pt-10">
			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					1. How Metro Manila Train Arrival Estimates Work (And Why Track Telemetry Is Closed)
				</h2>
				<div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						Commuters often wonder why Google Maps, Sakay.ph, or local transit websites do not show exact GPS train telemetry in Metro Manila the way systems in Tokyo, Singapore, or London do. Unlike cities that publish an open GTFS-Realtime (General Transit Feed Specification) stream straight from signaling computers, the Philippines' three major urban rail operators, DOTr MRT-3, Light Rail Manila Corporation (LRMC for LRT-1), and the Light Rail Transit Authority (LRTA for LRT-2), operate closed Automatic Train Protection (ATP) track-circuit networks without a public developer API.
					</p>
					<p>
						Because live train positions are kept internal to station control centers for operational safety, third-party Philippine commuter platforms use two different estimation techniques. Crowdsourced apps such as RushPH and TrainSight collect voluntary passenger location pings or manual platform check-ins to gauge train movement and congestion. Timetable engines, including this mapaPH estimator, rely on published first-train and last-train dispatch matrices combined with commercial cruise speeds (roughly 35 to 45 km/h inclusive of station braking) and dwell allowances (around 30 seconds per stop) to project when trains pass intermediate stations.
					</p>
					<p>
						For transparency, mapaPH explicitly distinguishes between scheduled cadence modeling and live GPS hardware tracking. If an unannounced unloading incident, power fluctuation, or maintenance speed restriction occurs along EDSA, Aurora Boulevard, or Taft Avenue, schedule math alone cannot detect the disruption. Riders should always consult official station PA announcements and operator social media advisories during severe rainy weather or yellow-alert speed restrictions.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					2. MRT-3 Extended Weekday Hours vs. Weekend Schedules
				</h2>
				<div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						One of the most frequent sources of late-night commuter confusion along EDSA stems from the difference between MRT-3's Monday-to-Friday extended evening service and its standard weekend hours. Effective March 24, 2025, the Department of Transportation extended weekday last trips by one full hour to serve BPO shift workers, university evening classes, and late retail employees across Quezon City, Mandaluyong, Makati, and Pasay.
					</p>
					<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs">
						<div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour">
							<h3 class="font-bold text-mapa-navy text-sm mb-2">Weekdays (Monday to Friday)</h3>
							<ul class="space-y-1.5 text-mapa-ink/80 list-disc pl-4">
								<li><strong>First Southbound (North Ave):</strong> Departs at 4:36 AM</li>
								<li><strong>First Northbound (Taft Ave):</strong> Departs at 5:18 AM</li>
								<li><strong>Last Southbound (North Ave):</strong> Departs at 10:30 PM (reaches Ayala ~10:56 PM)</li>
								<li><strong>Last Northbound (Taft Ave):</strong> Departs at 11:09 PM (reaches Cubao ~11:33 PM)</li>
							</ul>
						</div>
						<div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-mapa-canvas-dark border border-mapa-contour">
							<h3 class="font-bold text-mapa-ink text-sm mb-2">Saturdays, Sundays &amp; Holidays</h3>
							<ul class="space-y-1.5 text-mapa-ink/80 list-disc pl-4">
								<li><strong>First Southbound (North Ave):</strong> Departs around 4:38 AM</li>
								<li><strong>First Northbound (Taft Ave):</strong> Departs at 5:18 AM</li>
								<li><strong>Last Southbound (North Ave):</strong> Reverts earlier to 9:30 PM</li>
								<li><strong>Last Northbound (Taft Ave):</strong> Reverts earlier to 10:09 PM</li>
							</ul>
						</div>
					</div>
					<p>
						Notice the directional asymmetry: a northbound train leaving Taft Avenue at 11:09 PM takes roughly 32 minutes to travel the 16.9 km alignment back to North Avenue. That means commuters boarding northbound from Magallanes, Ayala, Buendia, Guadalupe, or Ortigas have significantly later final train clearance than riders waiting on the southbound platform coming from Quezon City.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					3. LRT-1 Cavite Extension (FPJ to Dr. Santos) &amp; LRT-2 Early Closure Rules
				</h2>
				<div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						With the opening of Cavite Extension Phase 1 extending LRT-1 southwards past Baclaran to Redemptorist, MIA Road, Asia World (PITX), Ninoy Aquino, and Dr. Santos (Sucat), the Green Line now spans 25 stations across nearly 24.5 kilometers. An end-to-end journey takes roughly 60 minutes during normal operation. Because trains take an hour to traverse the corridor, a last southbound trip departing Fernando Poe Jr. (formerly Roosevelt) at 10:45 PM on a weekday continues passing through Manila, Pasay, and Parañaque all the way past 11:30 PM before terminating at Dr. Santos.
					</p>
					<p>
						Conversely, LRT Line 2 (Purple Line from Recto to Antipolo) consistently maintains the shortest evening operating hours among Metro Manila's three rail lines. Stations open daily at 4:50 AM with initial trains rolling at 5:00 AM from both Recto and Antipolo, but the final westbound trip from Antipolo departs at <strong>9:00 PM</strong> and the final eastbound weekday trip from Recto leaves at <strong>9:30 PM</strong> (reverting to 9:00 PM on weekends and holidays).
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>The Late-Night Transfer Trap:</strong> Thousands of daily commuters switch between MRT-3 and LRT-2 at Araneta Center-Cubao, or between LRT-1 and LRT-2 via the Doroteo Jose to Recto covered footbridge. Even though MRT-3 runs past 10:30 PM on weekdays, arriving at Cubao after 9:15 PM means LRT-2 Eastbound service toward Katipunan, Marikina-Pasig, and Antipolo has likely already closed or is down to its final dispatch. Always check the connecting line's last train passing window before tapping in for a multi-line evening trip.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
					4. Station Gate Closure Buffers, Peak Headways &amp; Contingency Alternatives
				</h2>
				<div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-mapa-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						Knowing when the last train reaches your station is only half the equation. Across DOTr MRT-3, LRMC, and LRTA policies, ticket teller windows and entrance turnstile gates close <strong>3 to 5 minutes ahead</strong> of the actual final train departure to give passengers time to pass X-ray baggage inspection, purchase a Single Journey Ticket or tap a beep card, and climb to the platform safely. Arriving right on the minute of final departure typically results in locked station roller shutters.
					</p>
					<p>
						During normal weekday rush hours (6:00 AM to 9:00 AM and 5:00 PM to 8:00 PM), rail operators deploy maximum trainsets on the mainlines, yielding typical headways of <strong>3.5 to 4.5 minutes</strong> on MRT-3 and LRT-1, and <strong>4 to 5 minutes</strong> on LRT-2. Off-peak midday and weekend windows relax spacing to roughly 5 to 8 minutes as trainsets rotate for preventive inspection.
					</p>
					<p>
						<strong>What if you miss the last train?</strong> Along the entire EDSA corridor from Monumento through North Avenue, Cubao, Ortigas, Ayala, Taft Avenue, and PITX, the <a href="/tools/edsa-carousel" class="text-mapa-green font-bold hover:underline">EDSA Carousel Busway</a> continues operating <strong>24 hours a day, 7 days a week</strong> in dedicated center-median lanes. Meanwhile, along the Manila-Quezon City-Rizal Aurora Boulevard corridor served by LRT-2, traditional jeepneys and modern PUVs operate through the night at lower frequencies; use the <a href="/tools/fare-calculator" class="text-mapa-green font-bold hover:underline">LTFRB Commute Fare Calculator</a> to estimate overnight bus and jeepney fares.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Official Sources -->
